  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.105,
            0.207,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "PROBABILITY"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.862,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "203"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.139,
            0.774,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Are the outcomes of every experiment equally likely? Let us see."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.164,
            0.902,
            0.3
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Suppose that a bag contains 4 red balls and 1 blue ball, and you draw a ball without looking into the bag. What are the outcomes? Are the outcomes — a red ball and a blue ball equally likely? Since there are 4 red balls and only one blue ball, you would agree that you are more likely to get a red ball than a blue ball. So, the outcomes (a red ball or a blue ball) are not equally likely. However, the outcome of drawing a ball of any colour from the bag is equally likely. So, all experiments do not necessarily have equally likely outcomes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.306,
            0.902,
            0.344
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "However, in this chapter, from now on, we will "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "assume that all the experiments have equally likely outcomes.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.349,
            0.902,
            0.388
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Class IX, we defined the experimental or empirical probability P(E) of an event E as"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.217,
            0.398,
            0.752,
            0.441
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_1_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{E})=\\frac{\\text { Number of trials in which the event happened }}{\\text { Total number of trials }}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.445,
            0.903,
            0.606
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The empirical interpretation of probability can be applied to every event associated with an experiment which can be repeated a large number of times. The requirement of repeating an experiment has some limitations, as it may be very expensive or unfeasible in many situations. Of course, it worked well in coin tossing or die throwing experiments. But how about repeating the experiment of launching a satellite in order to compute the empirical probability of its failure during launching, or the repetition of the phenomenon of an earthquake to compute the empirical probability of a multi-storeyed building getting destroyed in an earthquake?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.611,
            0.902,
            0.712
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In experiments where we are prepared to make certain assumptions, the repetition of an experiment can be avoided, as the assumptions help in directly calculating the exact (theoretical) probability. The assumption of equally likely outcomes (which is valid in many experiments, as in the two examples above, of a coin and of a die) is one such assumption that leads us to the following definition of probability of an event."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.716,
            0.901,
            0.756
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "theoretical probability",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (also called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "classical probability",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ") of an event E, written as P(E), is defined as"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.77,
            0.816,
            0.814
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_1_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{P(E) = \\frac{ Number of outcomes favorable to E}{ Number of all possible outcomes of the experiment }}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.826,
            0.81,
            0.845
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "where we assume that the outcomes of the experiment are equally likely."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.85,
            0.718,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We will briefly refer to theoretical probability as probability."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.875,
            0.84,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This definition of probability was given by Pierre Simon Laplace in 1795."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.564,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
